WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.920
What does nondeterministic acceptance mean?

2
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.160
An automaton may have several

3
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.700
possible moves for a symbol,

4
00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:08.940
and epsilon moves consume no

5
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.700
input. Acceptance requires at least

6
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.220
one complete accepting branch, not

7
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.840
agreement among branches or a

8
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:16.880
favourable majority.

9
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.680
Use the four-state automaton from

10
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.840
the tutorial. State one has

11
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.460
an epsilon arrow to two,

12
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.340
two loops on b and

13
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:26.540
moves to three on a,

14
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.660
three has an epsilon arrow

15
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.320
to four, and four returns

16
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.020
to one on b. State

17
00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.080
one starts the computation and

18
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.740
only state four is accepting.

19
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.080
Before reading anything, follow any

20
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.400
number of epsilon arrows. The

21
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.860
active set is one and

22
00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.640
two, because epsilon reachability includes

23
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.420
taking zero moves as well

24
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.240
as taking the arrow. Neither

25
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.300
state is accepting, so this

26
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.540
automaton rejects the empty word.

27
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.480
Now read a. The branch

28
00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.260
at one has no a-transition

29
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.220
and dies, while the branch

30
00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.900
at two reaches three. Following

31
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.060
the epsilon arrow immediately adds

32
00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.920
four without advancing the input

33
00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.300
cursor a second time.

34
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.340
An accepting state is now

35
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.020
active, but b and a

36
00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:14.660
remain unread. This is not

37
00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:15.900
acceptance of the whole word

38
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.480
yet. Just as with a

39
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.440
deterministic automaton, a successful branch

40
00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.360
must finish the entire input

41
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.860
before ending in an accepting

42
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:24.340
state.

43
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.260
Read b next. State three

44
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.760
has no matching move, but

45
00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.560
four reaches one, and its

46
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.100
epsilon closure adds two. The

47
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.680
active set is therefore one

48
00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.100
and two again, with only

49
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.620
the final a unread.

50
00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.420
Read the final a. The

51
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.200
active set becomes three and

52
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.360
four after epsilon closure, and

53
00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.560
the input is exhausted. Since

54
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.140
four is accepting, the word

55
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:49.780
a b a is accepted,

56
00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.240
regardless of the branches that

57
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:51.960
died earlier.

58
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.020
Formally, the transition function returns

59
00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.200
a set of possible next

60
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.320
states. It may return the

61
00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.960
empty set, one state, or

62
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.740
several states, and its input

63
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.980
alphabet includes epsilon as a

64
00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.400
bookkeeping option. Epsilon is not

65
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.480
a character secretly present inside

66
00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:08.940
the word.

67
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.600
Why does flipping accepting states

68
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.260
fail to complement an arbitrary

69
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.100
nondeterministic automaton? A word can

70
00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.660
have one branch ending accepting

71
00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.440
and another ending nonaccepting. Flipping

72
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:21.780
the leaves can leave some

73
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.420
accepting branch in both machines,

74
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.340
so the word remains accepted

75
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:26.700
instead of changing its verdict.

76
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.660
Things to take note of.

77
00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.460
Apply epsilon reachability before the

78
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.880
first symbol, between consumed symbols

79
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.420
and after the last one.

80
00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.260
Keep all possible states, retire

81
00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.380
stuck branches, and accept only

82
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.140
when some surviving complete branch

83
00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.460
ends final; never count an

84
00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.480
epsilon move as consuming input.
